1. Data Management Module

This module handles the medical data available at different hospitals/healthcare institutions.

Input can include:

Patient demographic information
Medical history
Laboratory results
Symptoms
Clinical measurements
Disease labels

The important point is that raw patient data remains at the local institution



2. Data Preprocessing Module

Before training the model, each hospital preprocesses its own data.

Tasks include:

Removing duplicate/inconsistent records
Handling missing values
Encoding categorical data
Feature selection
Data normalization/scaling
Splitting data into training and testing sets

Raw Medical Data
       ↓
Cleaning
       ↓
Missing Value Handling
       ↓
Feature Engineering
       ↓
Normalization
       ↓
Training Dataset



3. Local Training Module

This is one of the most important modules.

Each hospital acts as a federated client/local node.

For example:
Hospital 1 → Local Model 1
Hospital 2 → Local Model 2
Hospital 3 → Local Model 3

Each hospital trains the model using its own patient data.

The raw data does not need to be sent to the central server.

Depending on the FedMed use case, the model could be:

Logistic Regression
Random Forest
SVM
Neural Network
CNN
Other appropriate ML/DL models


4. Model Update Sharing Module

After local training, each hospital sends model parameters/updates, rather than its raw medical records.
Hospital
   ↓
Local Training
   ↓
Updated Model Parameters
   ↓
Federated Server

This is what enables collaborative learning while reducing the need to centralize sensitive medical data.


5. Federated Server Module

The federated server coordinates the participating hospitals.

It:

Sends the current global model to clients.
Receives local model updates.
Aggregates the updates.
Produces a new global model.
Sends the improved model back to clients.

A common aggregation algorithm is FedAvg (Federated Averaging).

Conceptually:
Model 1 ─┐
Model 2 ─┤
Model 3 ─┼──→ FedAvg ──→ Global Model
Model N ─┘


6. Global Model & Evaluation Module

After aggregation, FedMed evaluates the global model.

Typical metrics include:

Accuracy
Precision
Recall
F1-score
ROC-AUC
Confusion Matrix

The process can be repeated for multiple federated rounds:

Global Model
     ↓
Send to Hospitals
     ↓
Local Training
     ↓
Collect Updates
     ↓
Aggregation
     ↓
New Global Model
     ↓
Evaluation
     ↓
Repeat


7. Medical Prediction Module

Once the global model reaches acceptable performance, it can be used for prediction.

For example:

Patient Information
        ↓
Preprocessing
        ↓
Trained Global Model
        ↓
Prediction
        ↓
Disease/Risk Result

Depending on the exact FedMed problem statement, the output could be a disease classification, risk score, or other medical prediction.


8. Dashboard / User Interface Module

The final module provides a simple interface for users such as researchers, healthcare professionals, or administrators.

The dashboard can display:

Patient input
Prediction
Confidence/probability
Model accuracy
Federated training rounds
Client performance
Global model performance
Evaluation metrics

For your implementation, Streamlit would be a relatively simple choice for the frontend.